    "q-143190": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143190",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Calculate $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}].$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $3+1=4$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,12\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 6+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(18+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{4} \\\\  & =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
